    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d293">

                <pb facs="0438" n="428" sameAs="frus:frus1945v07/pg428"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/7–745: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Hurley</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-07-07T21:00:00+07:00">July 7, 1945—9
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received July 7—1:25 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1127. Six members of the Committee, accompanied by General Wang Jo-fei, flew to
                    Yenan on July 1 (Embassy’s 1067, June 28, and 1081, June 30). The seventh
                    member, Mr. Wang Yun-wu, was prevented from going by illness. The 10[<hi
                        rendition="#italic">6?</hi>] members returned to Chungking on July 5.
                    General Wang did not accompany them, but they said he <pb facs="0439" n="429"
                        xml:id="pg429"/> was expected to return within a few days. (Sent to Dept.,
                    repeated to Moscow.)</p>
                <p>Dr. Fu Ssu-nien informed Smyth that the situation was “hopeful” but that he was
                    neither pessimistic nor optimistic. He added that a major obstacle appeared to
                    be the mutual lack of confidence between Kuomintang and Communists.</p>
                <p>The Generalissimo is meeting the Committee this evening and will receive from
                    them a document from the Communists brought back by the Committee. I expect to
                    be informed on the contents of the document tomorrow and will then communicate
                    further with the Dept.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hurley</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
